Dear Lisa,

I hope this email finds you well. I am writing to seek clarification and raise concerns regarding the denial of my vacation request, given the adjustments I have already made in good faith to accommodate team and business requirements.

Initially, I had planned a 6-week vacation (inclusive of 2 weeks unpaid leave), for which I made necessary bookings well in advance. Upon being informed by the manager that this duration did not align with company policy, I immediately revised my plans, reducing the length of my leave and incurring penalties for altering my travel arrangements.

Furthermore, I postponed my vacation to ensure coverage while a colleague was on leave, prioritizing the team’s needs and demonstrating my commitment to supporting business continuity. My current 4-week vacation plan, from February 10th to March 7th, reflects these adjustments.

However, I was recently informed that I am now required to return by the first week of March. This request creates a conflict with my already-rescheduled plans, despite my prior efforts to act as a team player and align with company policy.

I value my role and the trust placed in me to support the team’s goals, but I am concerned about the implications of this decision, especially given the financial and personal adjustments I have already made. I kindly request a review of this situation and ask whether there is flexibility to honor my approved 4-week vacation plan, which has been arranged to balance both my needs and the company’s requirements.

I am happy to discuss this further and explore any alternatives that might address business concerns while respecting the commitments I’ve made. Thank you for your understanding and support in resolving this matter.

Looking forward to your response.

Best regards,
Vidhi Soni

